 ### Canonical storage and identities
 
@@ -268,3 +301,20 @@ precompile cache identity, producer options, callable kernel ABI, and loader
 compatibility. Deserialization revalidates the expected request/build
 identities, Cubin SHA-256, strict producer contract, target, entry/parameter
 ABI, and byte-for-byte canonical encoding.
+
+Artifact identities repeat only the source digest and callable-ABI digest that
+the producer entrypoint checks directly, plus the complete request and
+KernelBuildSpec digests. Result, mutation, argument-catalog, and specialization
+digests remain caller-declared KernelBuildSpec inputs bound transitively by its
+complete digest; Artifact v1 does not present them as independently inferred or
+TensorIR-verified facts.
+
+`deserialize()` is deliberately a trusted-cache API. Producer identity and the
+Cubin SHA-256 are declarations inside the same self-contained payload; they
+provide deterministic identity and corruption detection, not cryptographic
+origin authentication. An attacker who controls cache bytes can replace them
+with another structurally valid ABI-compatible Cubin and recompute the hashes.
+Any cache that crosses a trust boundary must additionally use authenticated
+storage, verify a trusted externally supplied complete-Artifact digest or
+signature, or deterministically recompile. That authentication layer is a
+future cache transaction and is not claimed by Artifact v1.
